Writing $chi_(matbold(A))(t) = c_0 + c_1 t + dots.v + c_n t^n$, the leading coefficients are $c_n = (-1)^n$ and $c_(n-1) = (-1)^(n-1) tr(matbold(A))$. Comparing with the roots $lambda_1, ..., lambda_n$:

$ sum_(i = 1)^n lambda_i = tr matbold(A), quad product_(i = 1)^n lambda_i = chi_(matbold(A))(0) = det matbold(A), $

so an $n times n$ matrix has $n$ eigenvalues counted with multiplicity, and real matrices have eigenvalues that are either real or come in complex conjugate pairs.
